I have been consulted by a historian who doesn't like the way that
LaTeX splits footnotes (when the line containing the footnote marker
is the last non-footnote line, so that LaTeX must either move the line with
the marker to the next page or split the footnote).

My first response was to:
*  find 4 history books that split foonotes in just the same way as LaTeX
*  tell him that there are plenty of publishing precedents for what LaTeX does
*  ask why he wasn't pleased that LaTeX follows publishing precedents.

However, I've also found history books that don't seem to split footnotes
(I assume by design rather than by chance).  So I thought that it might be
worth seeing if LaTeX could be persuaded to try leaving a couple of
\baselineskips of white space
either at the bottom of the page
or     before the footnotes
rather than splitting a footnote.  I tried changing
\skip\footins 10pt plus 30pt minus 2pt
in rep11.sty, but this doesn't seem to help when the split footnote is
the only one on the page.  It looks as though
either \textheight would have to be a rubber length
or     a space, generally zero, would have to be added to the bottom
       of every text-column just in case it needs to stretch to avoid
       splitting a footnote
or     \footnote would have to be able to trigger a \clearpage.

It might be worthwhile if something was done in LaTeX 3.0 to make this
task easier (even if it's just a comment in the code somewhere saying
"If you want a design that prefers having ragged-bottom above footnotes to
having split footnotes, then ... ").

                                                                     David

